tep's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\n#### Alternative #1 — SequentialPipeline (confidence: 0.90)\nWhy this fits: Identical to the tree-class; this sequential approach allows the first stage to focus on the 'ingredients' and 'correctness' constraints while the second stage focuses on the 'iambic pentameter' constraint, fitting the draft-and-revise strength.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nSequentialPipeline is a :sequence of 2+ :llm/:code nodes where each step consumes the previous step's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\n#### Alternative #2 (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: This ETL pattern is effective for the 'ingredients' and 'steps' extraction/transformation. The strength 'use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage' is perfect for wrapping the final output in the requested JSON-like map format `{:ingredients [...] :steps [...] :yield \"24 cookies\"}`.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n#### Alternative #3 (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: Identical to the tree-class; uses sequential stages to manage the transition from raw recipe data to poetic steps, with a final formatting pass.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\nAssigned to domain child e2281910-609b-3df9-ab7a-a4a8e9d3dc83 (label: iambic-pentameter-recipe) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 2 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.80)\n   Why this fits: This task requires integrating two distinct structured modes (a prose ingredient list and a metered instructional sequence) into a single cohesive artifact (a recipe map). The candidate's recommended pattern for scaffolded synthesis via a code-built outline matches the need to preserve the recipe's correctness (accuracy constraint) while filling in the creative metered content section-by-section.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Synthesis integrates multiple structured inputs into a single cohesive artifact (narrative / report / recommendation). Always provide the synthesizer with inputs structured by source-id (not free-form concat) so attribution stays intact. For long artifacts, scaffold via a :code-built outline that the :llm fills section-by-section — preserves coverage. NEVER synthesize >5 substantive inputs in one call: do hierarchical clustering instead. Declare :output-schemas on the synthesis output when downstream stages (critique / validation) need to verify specific sections. Composes into briefing-generation, comparative-summary, and sequential-pipeline shells. Avoid when the task is ideation (new options) or critique (checking against criteria).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given multiple structured inputs (findings, extracts, source quotes), produce a single integrated artifact — narrative, report, recommendation\n  - natural fit at the tail of a tree where multiple parallel branches converge\n  - outputs are typically a single string (or small structured map) where the structure of the inputs has been collapsed into coherent prose\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** make the synthesizer's :reads contain the per-source extractions structured by source-id rather than a free-form concat — supports apples-to-apples reference and avoids the model losing track of which finding came from where (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: synthesis needs to attribute claims to sources OR balance per-source weight\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:narrative] :output-schemas {:narrative :string}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for long final artifacts, scaffold the synthesis with a :code node that pre-builds a structured outline from the inputs, then have the :llm fill each section — preserves coverage of all inputs (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final artifact is multi-section AND each section maps to a distinct cluster of inputs\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:findings] :writes [:outline] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:outline (mapv :type (:findings inputs))})}] [:llm {:reads [:findings :outline] :writes [:narrative]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing >5 distinct inputs in a single :llm call exceeds context — the model drops inputs from the middle of the context window silently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input count >5 AND each input is substantive\n    - Recommended fix: hierarchical synthesis: cluster inputs into 2-3 groups → per-group :llm synthesis → final :llm integrates the group narratives\n\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form synthesis output (no :output-schemas) makes downstream stages (critique / validation) work on unstructured prose — they can't easily check coverage or shape (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: downstream stages need to verify specific sections are present\n    - Recommended fix: declare a structured :output-schemas on the synthesis (e.g., :map with :summary :recommendations :open-questions) — downstream critique/validation can verify each named field\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - multi-source briefing: extracts from N articles → single executive summary with citations\n  - comparative report: per-vendor extractions → structured comparison + recommendation\n  - investigation conclusion: per-witness extractions → integrated timeline + assessment\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengt